Legal judgments (five items):
Market officials appraising commodity prices;
Buddhist and Daoist clergy doing obeisance to their parents;
Fraudulently obtaining travel permits by impersonation;
Restraining and binding persons by force and intimidation;
Penal-servitude convicts failing to perform their assigned labor.

Third Session

Policy questions:
Question: In the ages of Tang [Yao], Yu [Shun], and the Three Dynasties, the title "Veritable Records" (Shilu) did not yet exist; yet if one examines the texts of the canons, counsels, instructions, announcements, oaths, and charges [in the Book of Documents], the actual deeds of those emperors and kings can in general be seen. Coming down to the times of Han, Tang, and Song, the title "Veritable Records" first appeared. For instance, what is recorded in the Basic Annals, Hereditary Houses, and other sections of the Han histories is certainly detailed; why, then, did they not suffice for the governance of Han? For Gaozu, Taizong, and the other monarchs of Tang, their records were likewise complete; why, then, were they unable to record the affairs of Tang straightforwardly? Coming to the Song dynasty, the records became even more complete, the officials in charge more weighty, and their institutional status more exalted; as for the compilations across successive reigns, can you also speak of the compilers and their works? Vastly contemplating Our…
